---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import { dots } from '../lib/patterns.js';
const cardsBg = dots();

// Most builds span more than one service, so each card carries every tag that fits,
// and each tag links to the service page it belongs to.
const tag = {
  software: ['/software', 'Software solutions'],
  phone: ['/software#phone-apps', 'Phone apps'],
  assistants: ['/knowledge-assistants', 'AI system'],
  printing: ['/printing', '3D printing'],
};
---
<Base title="Products" description="What I make: FieldADE, an iPhone app for the AI system on your Mac; Home Base, the Mac app it talks to; Kingdom Guide, a Magic Kingdom day planner; a small-business site; and custom 3D prints.">
  <Hero variant="graph" seed={5} eyebrow="Products" title="Proof, not promises.">
    <p class="lede">Here are things I have built. Most cross more than one service, so each one is tagged with every service it shows.</p>
  </Hero>

  <section class="section band-parallax">
    <div class="band-bg" data-parallax="0.3" set:html={cardsBg} aria-hidden="true" />
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="grid">
        <div class="card work-card" id="fieldade">
          <h3>FieldADE</h3>
          <div class="work-shots">
            <img class="work-shot look-pro-only" src="/work/fieldade.png" alt="FieldADE on an iPhone: a chat with Claude, with a card asking to allow or deny a file edit" width="460" height="1000" loading="lazy" />
            <img class="work-shot look-dos-only" src="/work/fieldade-dos.png" alt="FieldADE on an iPhone: a chat with Claude, with a card asking to allow or deny a file edit, in the DOS look" width="460" height="1000" loading="lazy" />
            <img class="work-shot look-pro-only" src="/work/fieldade-list.png" alt="FieldADE's Agents screen: how much of each assistant's limit is used, and the list of open chats" width="460" height="1000" loading="lazy" />
            <img class="work-shot look-dos-only" src="/work/fieldade-list-dos.png" alt="FieldADE's Agents screen: how much of each assistant's limit is used, and the list of open chats, in the DOS look" width="460" height="1000" loading="lazy" />
          </div>
          <p>An iPhone app that reaches the AI assistants on my Mac from anywhere, over a private network. A chat app on your phone starts every answer from zero. These assistants run where my records live, next to my files, email, notes, and the vault that holds what I know about each job, and a knowledge assistant keeps all of it indexed. So from the truck I can ask what we quoted a customer last spring and get the quote, or ask for the follow-up and get it drafted from that thread. The records stay on the Mac; the phone only reaches them. Each chat runs in a folder, replies appear as they are written, and photos go along with a message. Before an assistant edits a file or runs a command, the phone buzzes and asks me to allow or deny it. I can change the app from the app: an assistant edits the code, checks it, and installs the new version on the phone. <a href="/map">See how it connects</a></p>
          <p class="tags">{[tag.phone, tag.software, tag.assistants].map(([href, label]) => <a class="tag" href={href}>{label}</a>)}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card work-card" id="home-base">
          <h3>Home Base</h3>
          <div class="work-shots work-shots-wide">
            <img class="work-shot-wide look-pro-only" src="/work/homebase.png" alt="Home Base on a Mac: a sidebar of screens, and the Agents screen showing how much of each assistant's limit is used and a list of open chats" width="1200" height="814" loading="lazy" />
            <img class="work-shot-wide look-dos-only" src="/work/homebase-dos.png" alt="Home Base on a Mac in the DOS look: a sidebar of screens, and the Agents screen showing how much of each assistant's limit is used and a list of open chats" width="1200" height="814" loading="lazy" />
          </div>
          <p>The Mac app FieldADE talks to. It runs the assistants on your Mac, takes each message from your phone, and sends the answers back. It opens a window with the same screens as the phone, so you can pick up any chat at the Mac where you left it on the phone, and a menu bar panel shows that it is running and pairs a new phone. It installs like any other app: no terminal, no setup scripts. Your files, email, and notes stay on the Mac; the phone only reaches them. <a href="/map">See how it connects</a></p>
          <p class="card-actions"><a class="btn btn-primary" href="https://downloads.embry.dev/HomeBase-latest.dmg" download>Download for Mac</a></p>
          <p class="fine">Version 0.1.0 &middot; macOS 14 or later &middot; signed and notarized by Apple. With Homebrew: <code>brew install --cask jtembry/fieldade/fieldade-home-base</code></p>
          <p class="tags">{[tag.software, tag.assistants].map(([href, label]) => <a class="tag" href={href}>{label}</a>)}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card work-card" id="kingdom-guide">
          <h3><a href="/kingdom-guide/support">Kingdom Guide</a></h3>
          <p>An iPhone app that plans a day at Magic Kingdom. It puts the rides a family picks in walking order, estimates when they will reach each one, and shows the posted waits. For families who use the Disability Access Service, it says which ride to book next and reminds them when the return time comes up. The next stop sits on the lock screen, the map is drawn from open map data, and everyone on the trip sees the same plan. It began as a web page for our own trip and is headed for the App Store. <a href="/kingdom-guide/support">About the app</a></p>
          <p class="tags">{[tag.phone, tag.software].map(([href, label]) => <a class="tag" href={href}>{label}</a>)}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card work-card" id="flourish">
          <p class="eyebrow">Small business</p>
          <h3><a href="https://flourishfinancialgroup.org">Flourish Financial Group</a></h3>
          <p>A financial counselor's site, live about a month after the first conversation. It is static and fast, costs $0 a month to host, and sits in her own GitHub account with a written guide, so she runs it herself.</p>
          <p class="tags">{[tag.software].map(([href, label]) => <a class="tag" href={href}>{label}</a>)}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card work-card" id="prints">
          <p class="eyebrow">3D printing</p>
          <h3><a href="/printing#prints">Featured prints</a></h3>
          <p>I design and print custom pieces for marketing and promotion: business cards, card holders, signs, and giveaways with your name on them. Each one starts as a short conversation and comes off my own printer. <a href="/printing#prints">See featured prints</a></p>
          <p class="tags">{[tag.printing].map(([href, label]) => <a class="tag" href={href}>{label}</a>)}</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .work-card { display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
  .work-card .tags { margin-top: auto; display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .4rem; }
  .work-card a.tag { text-decoration: none; }
  .work-card .card-actions { margin: 1rem 0 .5rem; }
  .work-card .fine { font-size: .85rem; color: var(--muted, inherit); margin: 0 0 1rem; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  .work-shots { display: flex; justify-content: center; gap: .75rem; margin: .25rem 0 1rem; }
  .work-shots-wide { margin: .25rem 0 1rem; }
  .work-shot-wide { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 10px; border: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .work-shot { display: block; flex: 1 1 0; min-width: 0; max-width: 200px; height: auto; border-radius: 22px; border: 1px solid var(--line); }
</style>
